From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Mar 1994 11:17:04 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-225] PCR: Is Certainty Relevant?

On Wed, 16 Mar 1994, Chris Phoenix wrote:

> > Another valid criticism of an idea, ceteris paribus, is its
> > usefulness and applicability. 
> 
>   I disagree with this.  Picture someone being brainwashed by a cult
> or political organization.  (I'm thinking specifically of the
> scenarios described by Jack Chalker in his "Spirits of Flux and
> Anchor" series.)  It may be that in a situation where a person is
> severely punished for showing signs of holding or not holding certain
> ideas, the most "useful" ideas are precisely the ones the people in
> power want the subject to have--and it doesn't really matter if the
> ideas contradict each other or not.

I'm being fairly punished for the crime of indulging in Latin. "Ceteris
paribus" means "all other things being equal". That means if you have two
different ideas you need to choose between that are both logically
consistent and that are both consistent with the data as revealed by the
best experiments you can design and can afford to carry out (or have read
about in the literature or whatever), *then* and only then do you turn to
less well-established critical methods, such as Ockham's Razor,
usefulness, and explanatory power. 

Logical inconsistency always matters. The effectiveness of brainwashing
techniques doesn't make this cease to matter, it just shows that it is
possible to force people to do things that are stupid, wrong, or contrary
to their own interests. No news there. 

>   By observation, I think some people do not use the law of
> noncontradiction, or at least not all the time--not if it would cause
> them to give up a cherished belief.  So then, the law of
> noncontradiction is not in their unstated criticisms. 

Well, people are not rational every second of the day. They are only 
rational when it counts. If what you are saying is relevant to PCR, then 
you must be saying that there are some people who reject the law of 
noncontraiction even when they are sitting down deliberately trying to 
figure things out and make decisions. 

This is not plausible. People who accepted that A is not A consistently
would be, among other things, gullible and easy to cheat, credulous, and
generally incompetent. In short, they would not be opponents worth
worrying about, and we could expect any genes they might have that would
predispose them to rejecting noncontradiction to have been weeded out a
long time ago. Ultimately, the evolution of knowledge is founded upon the
basis of biological evolution. Our original "theories" are the things that
we are biologically hardwired to intuit. (Mark Miller's discussion of
"creole physics" in the interview in EXTROPY #10 illustrates this
beautifully.) Likewise, our original set of critical techniques are those
that have been biologically hardwired into our intuition by natural
selection. Now, when I say "hardwired", I do not mean to imply that these
things are not subject to change, just that we have a predisposition for
them, that they accord with our intuitions about things. But we are
capable of discovering that our intuition is not always right, and
correcting for it when it is wrong. We have default programs, but they 
are not written in ROM.

What is more plausible is the idea of someone who believed that
noncontradiction applied in everyday circumstances, but not to specific
"special" circumstances. This is much the argument of the modern
Protestant theologians that Bartley is concerned with in THE RETREAT TO
COMMITMENT. In practice, such arguments always reduce to an explicit
rejection of rationality. The rejection of rationality may be condemned
but it cannot be argued against rationally. Such discussions ultimately
reduce to values, not to epistemological "axioms". 

>   So, in summary, is there anything about PCR that prevents people
> from starting with criticisms which strengthen themselves and weaken
> the case for more desirable (from our point of view) criticisms, to
> the point that two people may, while using PCR, end up with
> incompatible sets of criticisms?

In summary, no, there is nothing about PCR that prevents this. There is
however something about the evolved-by-natural-selection structure of our
brains which at least damps the variation in initial sets of plausible
criticisms. Even lower mammals have an approximate noncontradiction
heuristic wired into their brains, as well as other information-
manipulation structures each of which can provide a basis for a critical
heuristic. The reasons for this have have to do with survival and
stability, which serves as a pretty good rough-and-ready basis for
rationality. 

