>I'm saying a particle is to a brain as
>an individual is to a society. A brain is a group of particles. A
>society is a group of individuals.

Here's one way I've been thinking of the importance of the brain compared
to societies or markets:
                                     complexity of
   complexity of brain                social group 
  ----------------------    >    -----------------------    
  complexity of particle         complexity of individual 


You'd only need a few bits to specify what particle, so it approximates
to (complexity of individual)^2  >  complexity of social group.

Ralph Merkle's absolute maximum figure to specify the brain is 10^28 bits; 
this could be lowered to about 10^25 using a molecular rather than atomic
precision.  Hans Moravec's figures of 10^15 bits to encode a human brain
equivalent or 10^18 if you include body and immediate surroundings relies
on humans having been around to build the necessary hardware and discover
how to upload into cyberspace.  If the social group had a billion
individuals, allowing for features common to each individual, it would be
less than 10^9 times as complex as the individuals.

I've carried out some rough calculations here, doubtless in need of
revision.  What about the complexity of a market?  Let's say you had
100,000 different products (twice the number of words in a typical
dictionary, allowing for some permutations of adjective + noun).  And
each product is supplied by 20 competitors.  We'll say we need to specify
prices to an accuracy of 1%, and prices (taking some Schelling measurement
of weight, etc where necessary) could range from $0.01 to $1,000,000.
This needs about 232 possible prices per decade, over a 8-decade range.
So 1856 possible prices, 11 bits to specify price, and let's do the same
thing with supply and demand.  Better to be over-specified.  Each product
needs 33 bits plus 17 bits to specify the product, which is 50 bits for
2 million products.  So, 10^9 bits to specify the market.

What else -- product descriptions, product designs?  These might be
considered as memes in the brains of consumers and designers; if not it
still needs a lot to raise market complexity up to that of a brain.  

Still, the spontaneous order of the market does help people to become more
productive, and I expect the total extropy is in a way more than the sum
of its parts.  But the market doesn't seem to amplify complexity to the
same extent that the brain does, which has taken billions of years to
evolve.  Another way to look at it could be:

extropy generation         extropy generation
  of a brain                  of a market
------------------    >    ------------------      
extropy generation         extropy generation
  of a particle             of an individual

It would also be interesting to compare the complexity of "entropic"
non-living matter.  What would be the complexity of an "entropic"
snowcrystal, for instance?  We'll say it had to be specified to atomic
precision, within 0.01 nm, 10^-11 in 10^-3 m, or 1 in 10^8.  This needs
27 bits * 3 dimensions for each atom.  The brain at 10^6 times the volume
is about 10^26 atoms, so assuming similar density that leaves 10^20 atoms
times 81 bits, divide by 6 for redundancy of 6-sided symmetry gives about
10^21 bits.  More than I'd expected, probably over-specification.  And
once it's been produced, the snowcrystal can't generate any more extropy
like the brain or markets -- it can only join up with about two to three
dozen others in a disorderly way to form a snowflake. 

