We don't know for sure that the brain is just a universal Turing machine
or pre-von Neuman machine.  The Chinese Room argument didn't prove
anything either way, since Searle's opponents are correct in that the
lack of understanding of Chinese by Searle or other cogs in the machine is
irrelevent, and Searle's supporters are right in that mental functioning
is not necessarily limited to computation.  If I had a system made of a
hurricane and Turing machine, by simulating it I could only simulate the
computational part of it.  Qualia may be hardware dependent, and although
alternative constructions would work, e.g. replace meat parts with silicon,
it would be no good to simply simulate the particles.

I did a search on reductionism, and here's a reference I found, taken from
an on-line encyclopedia, on reductionism's reducing popularity amongst
psychologists (not to be distributed outside the list): 

----------------------------------------
  Psychology

      [...]         The  majority of psychologists are uninterested in such
traditional speculative questions as the relation of mind to body, although
there  has  been  a  considerable  opposition,  even  within  the  ranks of
experimental  psychology,  to  reductionism,  the  view  that psychological
events  are totally reducible to physical events. The vigour with which the
earlier experimental psychologists, such as J. B. Watson, rejected both the
introspectionist method and the study of conscious experience has given way
more  recently  to  the  increasingly  held view that 'internal' events are
major  phenomena  in  their  own  right and interact in important ways with
external  stimuli  to  determine  behaviour. The development of appropriate
techniques  for  studying  such  events  is  seen  as  both  necessary  and
desirable.
----------------------------------------

So how would my hardware dependent qualia producing brains work?

Let's say when I experience "green" a particular neuronal structure is
being 'fired' -- getting an electrochemical spiking.  One obvious
association with "green" is "fields", another is "trees", and some way
further down is "peas" and "Dr Who's Sea Devils".  So each of these is a
meme, which when fired creates the necessary qualia.  The interconnection
strength depends on the strength of the association, so when the green meme
fires, field also fires at a lower level, trees at a lower level again, and
peas and Dr Who sea devils at a lower level etc.  But it's the associations
that give meaning to each concept. 

It's more likely that qualia emerged because they confer a selective
advantage over zombies; thus they're unlikely to be epiphenomenal by-
products.

Now when the particles are being "simulated", there are no qualia, the
simulation is just being done on a machine.  I could make my computer
answer "yes" to the question: "Do you have a mind?", but this is no proof 
that it does.  If it walks like a duck, it needn't be a duck. 

