Macrocosmic irreversibility + microcosmic reversibility was regarded as a
paradox back in 1876.  Now the impossibility of observation of macrocosmic
behaviour at the microcosmic level conveniently acts as a sort of buffer
between the two levels, allowing both downward and upward causation with
no incompatibility between top-down and bottom-up.  I'll leave the
benefits of this for another time.

>> If the organization of the particles is important, doesn't this mean that
>> they must be put into place, and "simulating" them on a machine only gives
>> a simulation, not the true organization?
>
> No. What's important is what the organization of your particles do
>in relation to other's organization of particles. If a virtual set
>of particles perform the same actions as the real set, what's the difference?

If the virtual set perform other actions there is a difference.

>  I maintain that there is no difference between reality and a perfect
>simulation of it. If the whole universe were simulated, it would make
>absolutely no difference.

If the universe is real, then a simulation of a flame does not produce
its heat.  If the universe is simulated (which I think very unlikely)
reality could still be different.  We could be like dreamers who never
knew what being conscious was like, or like Plato's cave dwellers who'd
only ever seen shadows.  There could be a hierarchy of levels, so that by
the time you got up to about conscious-7, all people would have such
intensity of consciousness, feelings of purposiveness, desire to live, etc,
that command economies, deathism and philosophical behaviourism would be
rejected outright by all but the temporarily insane, and no one would even
consider that their free will might be just an illusion.

>  I would certainly agree that a command economy or government is more
>entropic , but for different reasons. I think the mind is a spontaneous
>order of parts of the brain just like a market economy is a spontaneous
>order of men. Command economies are entropic because they chose one
>path specifically instead of letting all paths compete and thus the
>command economy doesn't evolve, it stagnates and disorders itself
>relative to the market economy.

A command economy would be comparable to a brain with Alzheimer's disease,
schizophrenia or CJD, where the body's own "police force" white blood cells
attack nerve cells instead of granting them the negative right not to be
interfered with.  I like the idea of parts of the brain competing and an
eventual course of action being followed; the course of events in the free
market is like the purposive action of the free, healthy individual.  The
market has the advantage of being able to concentrate on its favorite
path, and simultaneously embark on its second favorite path but to a 
slightly lower extent, etc, due to being many men in many places.

>> As some examples, a crowd of football hooligans, a secret police force,
>> or a gang of drug-crazed muggers probably contains more entropy
>> together than any of them in isolation.
