From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Fri, 18 Mar 94 17:10:30 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-230] SCI: Inflation (was: PCR: Causality (Creation ...

Mike Price replies:
>> One thing about inflation that I haven't seen described or explained,
>> is just what it is that space inflates relative to.  (And "to what it
>> had been" is no answer!)
>
>[...]   For instance it takes light only approx 10 nanoseconds to
>cross a 3 meter gap.  Suppose (in some imaginary laboratory) we inflated
>a 3 meter cube of space by the 10^50 factor mentioned above and fired
>a laser beam through it.  Now the trip from one side to another takes
>approx 3.10^42 seconds or 30 billion trillion trillion years.

I make it nearer 10^42 seconds, still, a lot longer.  Over 10^24 times
the age of our universe.

>> If all of space was equally expanding, any measuring rods, observers'
>> heads, etc, would be expanding in exactly the same proportion; hence
>> would not notice anything.
>
>A measuring rod in a rapidly inflating/expanding regions would notice
>something.  At the rates suggested by Guth and others the rod would be
>ripped apart by geometric stresses.  In the lab example each subatomic
>particle from a measuring rod originally spanning the gap ends up
>separated from its nearest erstwhile neighbours by millions of trillion
>of lyrs.

This ripping apart sounds even more severe than the tidal effects an
unlucky traveller would experience at the event horizon of a low mass,
low radius black hole.  

>  We record the remnants of inflation in the Universe today -
>as the Hubble recession - precisely because the size of bound systems
>like atomic electron orbits and galactic radii etc, in common with the
>speed of light and other constants, do not inflate with the Universe.

The part I find unexplained is how these bound systems 'know' that their
radii shouldn't inflate even when the space they occupy is inflating or
expanding.  There are ways of explaining this, e.g. having a few extra
dimensions which stay fixed, soon becoming greatly squashed compared to
the inflating dimensions.  In the 11-dimensional universe models, the
electromagnetic and nuclear forces are warps in the spare seven space
dimensions, which would explain how the range of these fields doesn't
expand with 4D space, hence the atoms don't expand.  But with gravity a
characteristic of the more familiar 4 dimensions, this doesn't seem to
explain the galactic radii's refusal to expand with normal spacetime. 

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

