From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 19 Mar 94 10:22:26 GMT
Subject: [#94-3-233] PCR: Relative Boundary of Certainty

(I sent this twice but the list seemed to be down - bounced back.)
Reilly Jones wrote on 13 Mar 94:
>Steve Whitrow wrote 3/12/94: <Some 'certainties' are relative, but these are
>the absolutists' 'certainties', not the PCRists' certainties.  Therefore
>everyone is certain that certainty is not relative, according to their own
>definitions.  But the class of things that are 'certainties' varies from one
>person to the next, so the boundary of certainty is relative.  And everyone
>is certain that belief is relative.>
>
>In the table of certainties for PCR/evolutionary epistemologies, only "I am"
>and "I think" were listed.  These are strictly subjective reality certainties,
>is PCR only capable of subjective certainties?  How can you be certain of "I
>am" and "I think" without being certain of "Existence exists" which certainly
>must precede these two certainties within subjective reality?  Or do you
>equate exactly the subjective certainty "I am" with "Existence exists."

It depends what is meant by "existence exists".  I decided to start with
the most basic certainties -- "I think" and "I am", and these I see as
statements about objective reality as well as subjective reality.  However,
they tell us hardly anything about objective reality.  Only that "I am",
some sort of thing exists that I call "I", and "I think", which refers to
a process, some event that "I think" refers to.  I might even be a
simulation, with my "thinking" in reality being merely the workings of a
child's toy.

(I think it's fair to reject radical idealism as a possibility since it
could never be proven true.  Even if magickal things started happening in
the world, pigs flying, etc, it would be better to assume that either I
was hallucinating and there is a material world, or I'm being simulated in
cyberspace and there is a material world that the simulators inhabit.  In
any case, even a "mind in a mental world" must be some sort of thing plus
processes -- if there was no material world how could you tell that the
mental world was not "material"?  Its natural laws might be erratic, but
this would be no proof.  The existence of a "mental world" seems to
presuppose the existence of a material world, in order that the mental
world may be immaterial.)

So I can assume "there is a material world" and safely say I could never
be proved wrong.  But I could not be sure that I'm not being simulated; I
might wake up tomorrow in the simulators' world and find that up to now
everything's been an illusion.  It's extremely unlikely, though.

As for "existence exists", in one interpretation it's a ridiculous
tautology which could never be falsified, so this isn't what I meant by it.
Rather, if existence exists, then what we consider to be the material
world, "existence", is a reasonable approximation to reality.  If not,
it's a simulation, and we're cyberspace simulations, brains in vats, or
whatever really causes subjective events.  So this is a proposition which
could in principle be falsified.  The non-PCR side seem to take this
"existence exists" as an axiom, whereas PCRists would give it an
exceedingly high credibility, possibly a probability of 1 - 10^-20,
although I'd like to know if this is regarded as too high.  In subjective
reality though, you could be certain that "I am not a simulation /
existence exists" is an extremely credible belief. 

If "existence exists" is really the tautological version and "A is A" is
supposed to be how I defined "existence exists", then "existence exists"
would become a certainty for both sides, with "A is A" taken as a
certainty for non-PCRists and a belief for PCRists.  

(Of course, you might say that "I am" is also a ridiculous tautology which
could never be falsified; I picked the more sensible version of "existence
exists" but I don't know another interpretation of "I am".)

>The epistemological boundaries evolve over time as we
>push on them.  Because of changes occurring in these boundaries, in purposes,
>and as a result of PCR-type processes, the point of coupling between the
>certainty of the boundary and the certainty of truth changes.  This change in
>the point of coupling between these certainties is what I believe you are
>referring to when you say "the boundary of certainty is relative."  The
>coupling between these two certainties still produces a single certainty,
>i.e., not relative, even as the point of coupling changes over time.

I meant that the class of things that are certainties is not absolute but
varies from one person to another.  PCRists would have a very small number
of objective reality certainties but would be constantly increasing and
developing their extremely high credibility beliefs, and constantly
becoming more accurate in rating the credibility of each belief.
Empiricists, etc, would seem to increase their axioms, but every now and
then an "axiom" would be proved false -- not very good for credibility.  

>I applaud your effort to construct a table of beliefs vs. certainties for PCR.
>If you and Eric Forste (and anyone else) would expand on this table, perhaps I
>could better understand how PCR is different from other prescriptions for
>rationality.

In PCR there would be many very high credibility beliefs; certainties would
have to be fairly tautological and impossible to falsify, e.g. "no lights
are black".  With the possibility of uplifted beasts, I doubt if you could
have "no horses are men" in PCR.  I think "there is a material world" would
be ok.

